window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-bio-sexual-repro-plants","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants · Practice","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The term 'monosporic development' of the female gametophyte means that:","image":"","options":["Only one megaspore mother cell is present in the ovary","The embryo sac develops from a single functional megaspore while the other three degenerate","Only one male gamete takes part in fertilisation","The megaspore mother cell develops directly into the embryo sac without meiosis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In most flowering plants, one of the four megaspores is functional and forms the embryo sac, while the other three degenerate. Development from a single megaspore is what 'monosporic' means.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assume every embryo sac develops monosporically and each seed results from one double fertilisation. What is the minimum number of meiotic divisions needed to produce 100 viable seeds in an angiosperm?","image":"","options":["100","125","150","200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each embryo sac comes from one megaspore mother cell, so 100 seeds need 100 meioses on the female side. One pollen grain fertilises one embryo sac and one pollen mother cell gives 4 pollen grains, so 25 more meioses are needed on the male side, for 125 in total. The trap is counting 2 male gametes per seed as needing separate pollen grains; both gametes come from the same pollen grain.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The cleistogamous flowers of Viola, Oxalis and Commelina produce assured seed-set even when pollinators are absent.\nReason (R): In cleistogamous flowers, the anthers dehisce inside the unopened flower and release pollen onto the stigma lying close to them.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Cleistogamous flowers never open, and their anthers and stigma lie close together. Pollen released inside the bud lands directly on the stigma, so pollination needs no external agent and seed-set is assured. R therefore directly explains A. Do not confuse these with the chasmogamous flowers of the same plants, which open and expose their anthers and stigma.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about embryo development in angiosperms is incorrect?","image":"","options":["A typical dicot embryo has an embryonal axis and two cotyledons.","The root tip of a dicot embryo is covered with a root cap.","The early stages of embryogeny differ completely between monocotyledons and dicotyledons.","In grasses, the radicle and root cap are enclosed in the coleorrhiza."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Seeds of monocots and dicots differ greatly, but the early stages of embryogeny are similar in both groups. The other three statements agree with the structure of dicot and grass embryos.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student dusts pollen of pea or Vinca on a glass slide to watch pollen tubes emerge under the low power of a microscope. What should the drop on the slide contain?","image":"","options":["Distilled water only","About 10 per cent sugar solution","About 10 per cent common salt solution","Dilute hydrochloric acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pollen of pea, chickpea, Crotalaria, balsam and Vinca germinates in a drop of about 10% sugar solution. Pollen tubes become visible after 15–30 minutes. A salt solution or acid would not support germination.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Transfer of pollen grains from the anther of one flower to the stigma of another flower borne on the same plant is called:","image":"","options":["Autogamy","Xenogamy","Geitonogamy","Cleistogamy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Geitonogamy is pollination between different flowers of the same plant. Xenogamy needs a different plant, and autogamy happens within a single flower.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In an angiosperm, each cell of the nucellus contains 24 chromosomes. What will be the chromosome numbers in the zygote, the primary endosperm nucleus and a synergid, respectively?","image":"","options":["24, 24, 12","12, 36, 24","24, 36, 12","24, 36, 24"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The nucellus is diploid sporophytic tissue, so 2n = 24 and n = 12. The synergid belongs to the haploid embryo sac and has 12 chromosomes. The zygote (male gamete + egg) is diploid with 24, and the PEN (male gamete + two polar nuclei) is triploid with 36.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The water of a tender coconut is free-nuclear endosperm.\nReason (R): In the most common type of endosperm development, each division of the primary endosperm nucleus is immediately followed by cell wall formation.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Coconut water contains thousands of free nuclei, while the surrounding white kernel is cellular endosperm. In the common type of development, the PEN first divides repeatedly without walls (free-nuclear stage), and cellularisation comes later, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about Panchanan Maheshwari are correct?\n(i) He was born in Jaipur in 1904.\n(ii) His work on test tube fertilisation and intra-ovarian pollination won worldwide acclaim.\n(iii) He emphasised the need to begin work on artificial culture of immature embryos.\n(iv) He established the Department of Botany, University of Allahabad, as a centre of embryology research.\n(v) He was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society of London.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iii) and (v) are correct. He studied at Allahabad, but the department he built into a centre of embryology and tissue culture was at the University of Delhi, so (iv) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following aquatic plants is NOT pollinated by water? Its flowers emerge above the water surface and are pollinated by insects or wind.","image":"","options":["Vallisneria","Hydrilla","Zostera","Water hyacinth"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Water hyacinth and water lily raise their flowers above the water and are pollinated by insects or wind, like most land plants. Vallisneria, Hydrilla and the sea-grass Zostera are true water-pollinated plants. Living in water does not mean a plant is water-pollinated.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Each cell of the sporogenous tissue is a potential pollen mother cell.\n(ii) Germ pores are regions of the exine where sporopollenin is absent.\n(iii) The cells of a microspore tetrad are haploid.\n(iv) The intine is discontinuous at the germ pores.\n(v) In a mature pollen grain, the generative cell is larger than the vegetative cell.\n(vi) Microspores are formed from the pollen mother cell by mitosis.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iii) are correct, giving three. The intine is a continuous layer, the generative cell is the smaller cell, and microspores form from the PMC by meiosis, so (iv), (v) and (vi) are wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is a false fruit, in which the thalamus also contributes to fruit formation?","image":"","options":["Mango","Guava","Strawberry","Orange"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In apple, strawberry and cashew, the thalamus takes part in forming the fruit, so they are false fruits. Mango, guava and orange develop only from the ovary, so they are true fruits.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a mature seed, the micropyle persists as a small pore in the seed coat. What is its role?","image":"","options":["It is the site where the pollen tube enters during fertilisation of the seed","It allows oxygen and water to enter the seed during germination","It is the point where the seed is attached to the fruit wall","It releases stored food from the endosperm to the cotyledons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The micropylar pore in the seed coat lets oxygen and water enter the seed during germination. Pollen tube entry happens through the micropyle of the ovule before the seed forms, not in the mature seed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a tender coconut, the coconut water and the surrounding white kernel represent, respectively:","image":"","options":["Cellular endosperm and free-nuclear endosperm","Free-nuclear endosperm and cellular endosperm","Free-nuclear endosperm and perisperm","Nucellus and cellular endosperm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Coconut water is free-nuclear endosperm made of thousands of free nuclei. The white kernel is the endosperm after cell walls have formed, i.e., cellular endosperm. Perisperm is residual nucellus, which is not what the kernel is.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A typical angiosperm anther is described as tetrasporangiate.\nReason (R): A typical anther is bilobed and each lobe is monothecous.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The anther does have four microsporangia, so A is true. R is false because each lobe has two theca, which makes the anther dithecous, not monothecous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants are pollinated by water?\nVallisneria, Hydrilla, Zostera, water hyacinth, water lily","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Vallisneria, Hydrilla and the sea-grass Zostera are water-pollinated. Water hyacinth and water lily are aquatic, but their flowers rise above the water and are pollinated by insects or wind.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants have many ovules in a single ovary?\nWheat, Papaya, Paddy, Orchids, Mango, Water melon","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Papaya, orchids and water melon have many ovules per ovary. Wheat, paddy and mango have only one ovule per ovary, so the count is 3.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following shows the correct sequence of stages in the development of a dicotyledonous embryo from the zygote?","image":"","options":["Zygote → Globular embryo → Proembryo → Heart-shaped embryo → Mature embryo","Zygote → Proembryo → Globular embryo → Heart-shaped embryo → Mature embryo","Zygote → Proembryo → Heart-shaped embryo → Globular embryo → Mature embryo","Zygote → Heart-shaped embryo → Proembryo → Globular embryo → Mature embryo"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The zygote, located at the micropylar end of the embryo sac, first forms the proembryo. The proembryo becomes globular, then heart-shaped, and finally matures. A common trap is placing the heart-shaped stage before the globular stage.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the stamen and anther are correct?\n(i) The anther is a tetragonal structure with four microsporangia, two in each lobe.\n(ii) The outer three wall layers of the microsporangium protect it and help in dehiscence of the anther.\n(iii) In a young anther, the centre of each microsporangium is occupied by compactly arranged homogenous sporogenous cells.\n(iv) Tapetal cells have sparse cytoplasm and are always uninucleate.\n(v) The proximal end of the filament is attached to the thalamus or the petal of the flower.\n(vi) Mature microsporangia become pollen sacs that are confined to the tip of the anther.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iii) and (v) are correct. Statement (iv) is wrong because tapetal cells have dense cytoplasm and generally more than one nucleus. Statement (vi) is wrong because pollen sacs extend longitudinally along the whole length of the anther.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The gynoecium of Michelia is multicarpellary.\nReason (R): In Michelia, the pistils are fused together to form a single compound pistil.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Michelia does have many pistils, so A is true. However, those pistils are free from one another (apocarpous), not fused, so R is false. Fused pistils are seen in Papaver.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Geitonogamy is functionally a type of cross-pollination because it involves a pollinating agent.\nStatement II: Geitonogamy brings genetically different pollen grains to the stigma.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Geitonogamy needs an agent to carry pollen between flowers, but the pollen comes from the same plant, so genetically it is like autogamy. Only xenogamy brings genetically different pollen.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In most angiosperms, the zygote divides only after a certain amount of endosperm has been formed.\nReason (R): This delay ensures assured nutrition for the developing embryo.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Endosperm development comes before embryo development, so the endosperm is ready to feed the embryo. The zygote's delay in dividing is an adaptation for exactly this assured nutrition, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the triploid endosperm of a typical angiosperm, what is the ratio of chromosome sets contributed by the female parent to those contributed by the male parent?","image":"","options":["1 : 2","1 : 1","3 : 0","2 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The PEN forms from two polar nuclei, which belong to the female gametophyte, and one male gamete. So the endosperm carries two maternal sets and one paternal set. The trap is assuming an equal 1 : 1 contribution, which holds only for the zygote.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Nucellar embryos in Citrus are genetically identical to the mother plant.\nReason (R): Nucellar cells are diploid cells of the parent that divide and develop into embryos without meiosis or fertilisation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Nucellar cells belong to the maternal sporophyte. They form embryos with no reduction division and no gamete fusion, so these embryos are clones of the mother plant. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A seed of an orange variety contains several embryos. One embryo came from the fertilised egg, and the others came from nucellar cells that protruded into the embryo sac. Which statement about the nucellar embryos is correct?","image":"","options":["They are haploid because they arise inside the embryo sac","They are triploid because they develop alongside the endosperm","They are diploid and genetically identical to the mother plant, so they behave as clones","They are genetically identical to the plant that supplied the pollen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Nucellar cells are diploid sporophytic tissue of the mother plant, and they form embryos without meiosis or fertilisation, so these embryos are maternal clones. Only the zygotic embryo carries genes from both parents.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"When a seed of orange is squeezed, several embryos of different sizes and shapes come out. This phenomenon is called:","image":"","options":["Parthenocarpy","Polyembryony","Perisperm formation","Cleistogamy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Polyembryony is the occurrence of more than one embryo in a seed. In Citrus it results from nucellar embryos. Perisperm is persistent nucellus, not extra embryos.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"After the pollen tube enters the embryo sac, where are the two male gametes discharged?","image":"","options":["Into the central cell, directly beside the polar nuclei","Into the cytoplasm of one of the synergids","Into the egg cell","Into one of the antipodal cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The pollen tube enters one synergid and releases both male gametes into its cytoplasm. From there, one gamete moves to the egg and the other moves to the polar nuclei in the central cell.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In angiosperms, development of the endosperm precedes development of the embryo.\nReason (R): The primary endosperm cell is diploid, formed by the fusion of one male gamete with a single polar nucleus.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Endosperm forms first so that the developing embryo has assured nutrition. However, the primary endosperm nucleus is triploid, because a male gamete fuses with two polar nuclei (triple fusion), so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Vallisneria, female flowers reach the water surface on long stalks, while male flowers or pollen grains are released onto the surface and carried passively by water currents.\nStatement II: Water hyacinth and water lily are typical examples of water-pollinated plants.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I correctly describes pollination in Vallisneria. Not all aquatic plants are water-pollinated: water hyacinth and water lily raise their flowers above the water, where insects or wind pollinate them.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cleistogamous flowers produce assured seed-set even in the absence of pollinators.\nReason (R): Cleistogamous flowers never open, and their anthers dehisce inside the bud close to the stigma.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Cleistogamous flowers stay closed and their anthers lie near the stigma, so pollen from the bud reaches the stigma directly. Because no external agent is needed, seed-set is assured, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical angiosperm ovule, the megaspore mother cell (MMC) usually differentiates in which region?","image":"","options":["Chalazal region of the nucellus","Micropylar region of the nucellus","Inner integument near the funicle","Hilum, at the junction of ovule and funicle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ovules generally form a single large MMC with dense cytoplasm in the micropylar region of the nucellus. It arises within the nucellus, not in the integuments or at the hilum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Pollen grains are well preserved as fossils.\nReason (R): The exine contains sporopollenin, which withstands high temperatures and strong acids and alkalis, and no enzyme that degrades it is known so far.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Sporopollenin is extremely resistant to heat, chemicals and enzymes, so pollen grains survive as fossils. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A stamen consists of a filament and an anther, and the androecium is a whorl of stamens that represents the male reproductive organ.\nStatement II: The outer three wall layers of a microsporangium are mainly responsible for nourishing the developing pollen grains.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Statement II is wrong because the epidermis, endothecium and middle layers protect the microsporangium and help in dehiscence, while nourishment is the role of the tapetum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Coconut water is free-nuclear endosperm, and the white kernel is cellular endosperm.\n(b) In pea and groundnut, the endosperm is consumed before seed maturation.\n(c) Castor seeds are albuminous.\n(d) The zygote usually divides before any endosperm is formed.\n(e) The pericarp develops from the wall of the ovary.\n(f) Perisperm is the residual endosperm persisting in some seeds.\n(g) The micropyle persists as a pore in the seed coat and allows entry of oxygen and water during germination.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (b), (c), (e) and (g) are correct, giving five. Endosperm development precedes zygote division, so (d) is wrong. Perisperm is persistent nucellus, not endosperm, so (f) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Epicotyl\n(B) Coleoptile\n(C) Coleorrhiza\n(D) Scutellum\nList-II\n(I) Undifferentiated sheath enclosing the radicle and root cap\n(II) Lateral cotyledon of the grass embryo\n(III) Terminates in the plumule\n(IV) Hollow foliar structure enclosing the shoot apex and leaf primordia\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The epicotyl ends in the plumule. The coleoptile is a hollow foliar sheath over the shoot apex and leaf primordia. The coleorrhiza sheathes the radicle and root cap, and the scutellum is the lateral cotyledon. Confusing coleoptile with coleorrhiza is the common error.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The hard outer wall layer of a pollen grain is made of a material that withstands high temperatures, strong acids and alkalis. No enzyme is known to degrade it. This material is:","image":"","options":["Cellulose","Pectin","Sporopollenin","Lignin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The exine is made of sporopollenin, one of the most resistant organic materials known. Cellulose and pectin form the inner intine, not the exine.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flowering plant has 2n = 24 chromosomes. In its developing anther, what is the chromosome number in a cell of the endothecium and in a single cell of a microspore tetrad?","image":"","options":["Endothecium 12; microspore 12","Endothecium 24; microspore 24","Endothecium 24; microspore 12","Endothecium 12; microspore 24"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The endothecium is a somatic wall layer, so its cells are diploid (24). Microspore tetrads form by meiosis of the sporogenous cells (pollen mother cells), so each microspore is haploid (12).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Germ pore\n(B) Vegetative cell\n(C) Generative cell\n(D) Intine\nList-II\n(I) Spindle shaped, with dense cytoplasm, floats in the cytoplasm of another cell\n(II) Region of exine where sporopollenin is absent\n(III) Thin, continuous layer of cellulose and pectin\n(IV) Larger cell with abundant food reserve and an irregularly shaped nucleus\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Germ pores are gaps in the exine where sporopollenin is absent. The vegetative cell is large, food-rich and has an irregular nucleus. The small, spindle-shaped generative cell floats inside it, and the intine is the cellulose–pectin layer.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the intine of a pollen grain?","image":"","options":["Thick, discontinuous layer of sporopollenin interrupted by germ pores","Thin, continuous layer made of cellulose and pectin","Thin layer of sporopollenin lying beneath the plasma membrane","Thick layer of pectin found only at the germ pores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The intine is the inner wall layer. It is thin, continuous and made of cellulose and pectin. The germ pores are gaps in the exine, where sporopollenin is absent.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In castor and maize, the production of unisexual flowers prevents autogamy but not geitonogamy.\nReason (R): In these plants, both male and female flowers are borne on the same plant.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Castor and maize are monoecious. Unisexual flowers rule out pollination within the same flower. However, because male and female flowers are on the same plant, pollen can still pass from one flower to another on that plant, so R explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following aquatic plants is NOT pollinated by water?","image":"","options":["Vallisneria","Hydrilla","Zostera","Water hyacinth"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The flowers of water hyacinth (and water lily) rise above the water surface and are pollinated by insects or wind, as in land plants. Vallisneria, Hydrilla and Zostera are true water-pollinated plants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram of a mature angiosperm embryo sac shows a cell at the micropylar end, beside the egg cell. Its micropylar tip bears special cellular thickenings. What is this cell, and what is the role of the thickenings?","image":"","options":["Antipodal cell; the thickenings nourish the embryo sac","Central cell; the thickenings hold the polar nuclei in place","Synergid; the thickenings (filiform apparatus) guide the pollen tube into it","Egg cell; the thickenings help it fuse with the male gamete"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The two synergids flank the egg at the micropylar end, and their filiform apparatus guides pollen tube entry. Antipodals lie at the chalazal end, and the central cell holds the two polar nuclei.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student describes the positions of various parts of mature embryos relative to the cotyledon(s). Which of the following descriptions is NOT correct?","image":"","options":["Plumule – at the tip of the axis portion lying above the level of the cotyledons in a dicot","Radicle – at the lower end of the hypocotyl in a dicot","Coleoptile – enclosing the shoot apex on the axis portion above the scutellum's attachment in a grass","Scutellum – located at the terminal tip of the embryonal axis in a grass"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The scutellum is attached laterally, to one side of the embryonal axis, not at its tip. The other three positions are correct: the plumule tips the epicotyl, the radicle ends the hypocotyl, and the coleoptile covers the shoot apex above the scutellum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If the female parent in a crossing experiment produces unisexual flowers, emasculation is not required.\nStatement II: In such a female parent, the flowers are bagged only after they open and their stigmas become receptive.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Female unisexual flowers have no anthers, so emasculation is unnecessary. However, the female flower buds must be bagged before the flowers open. Waiting until the stigma is receptive would allow contamination by unwanted pollen.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Amorphophallus\nB. Yucca\nC. Vallisneria\nD. Zostera\nList-II\nI. Female flowers remain submerged; pollen released inside water\nII. Female flower reaches water surface by a long stalk\nIII. Floral reward is a safe place to lay eggs; flower about 6 feet tall\nIV. Obligate mutualism with a moth species\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Amorphophallus, the tallest flower, offers egg-laying sites as its reward, and Yucca depends on a moth. In Vallisneria the female flower rises to the surface on a long stalk, while seagrasses like Zostera release pollen under water to submerged female flowers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A self-incompatible species bears bisexual flowers. A bee carries pollen from one flower to the receptive stigma of another flower on the same plant. What is the most likely outcome?","image":"","options":["Seeds are formed, because geitonogamy is functionally cross-pollination involving an agent","Seeds are formed, because self-incompatibility blocks only pollen from the same flower","No seeds are formed, because the pistil inhibits germination or tube growth of pollen from the same plant","No seeds are formed, because the pollen tube enters through the chalaza instead of the micropyle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Self-incompatibility blocks self-pollen from the same flower and also from other flowers of the same plant. Geitonogamy is genetically similar to autogamy, so the pistil rejects this pollen. Its functional resemblance to cross-pollination is the trap here.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In over 60 per cent of angiosperms, pollen grains are shed at the 2-celled stage, containing a vegetative cell and a generative cell.\nStatement II: In species that shed 3-celled pollen, the two male gametes arise by meiotic division of the generative cell before shedding.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Most angiosperms (over 60%) shed pollen at the 2-celled stage, so Statement I is correct. In 3-celled pollen the generative cell divides mitotically, not meiotically, so Statement II is incorrect; the gametophyte is already haploid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Lupinus arcticus\nB. Phoenix dactylifera\nC. Banana\nD. Cashew\nList-II\n(i) Thalamus also contributes to fruit formation\n(ii) Fruit develops without fertilisation\n(iii) Viable seed about 2000 years old recovered near the Dead Sea\n(iv) Seed germinated after an estimated 10,000 years of dormancy in Arctic Tundra\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The lupine seed from the Arctic Tundra holds the 10,000-year record. The date palm seed from King Herod's palace near the Dead Sea was about 2000 years old. Banana is parthenocarpic, and cashew is a false fruit because its thalamus contributes to the fruit; the usual trap is swapping the two viability records.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following outbreeding conditions prevents both autogamy and geitonogamy?","image":"","options":["Monoecy, as in maize","Monoecy, as in castor","Dioecy, as in papaya","Cleistogamy, as in Commelina"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In dioecious plants such as papaya, each plant bears only male or only female flowers, so pollen from the same plant cannot reach a stigma. Monoecy prevents only autogamy, while cleistogamy actually ensures autogamy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following have a mature seed in which part of the endosperm persists?\nCastor, Pea, Coconut, Beans, Wheat, Groundnut, Maize","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Castor, coconut, wheat and maize retain endosperm in the mature seed (albuminous seeds), which makes four. Pea, beans and groundnut are non-albuminous because their endosperm is fully consumed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In most water-pollinated species, the pollen grains are protected from wetting by:","image":"","options":["A thick layer of sporopollenin in the intine","A mucilaginous covering","A waxy cuticle secreted by the tapetum","Air sacs attached to the exine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A mucilaginous covering keeps the pollen of most water-pollinated plants from getting wet. Sporopollenin is found in the exine, not the intine.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Exine\nB. Intine\nC. Vegetative cell\nD. Generative cell\nList-II\nI. Spindle-shaped, dense cytoplasm, floats in the cytoplasm of another cell\nII. Thin, continuous layer of cellulose and pectin\nIII. Bigger cell with abundant food reserve and a large irregular nucleus\nIV. Resistant layer of sporopollenin with apertures\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The exine is the sporopollenin layer with germ pores, and the intine is the thin cellulose–pectin layer. The vegetative cell is the large, food-rich cell, while the small spindle-shaped generative cell floats in its cytoplasm.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assuming monosporic embryo sac development and that every microspore forms a pollen grain, what is the minimum number of meiotic divisions needed to produce 100 pollen grains and 100 embryo sacs, respectively?","image":"","options":["25 and 100","100 and 25","25 and 25","100 and 100"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"One meiosis of a pollen mother cell gives four functional microspores, so 100 pollen grains need 25 meioses. One meiosis of an MMC yields only one functional megaspore, so 100 embryo sacs need 100 meioses.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about a typical angiosperm embryo sac.\n(a) The functional megaspore nucleus undergoes three successive free-nuclear mitotic divisions to form eight nuclei.\n(b) The three antipodal cells are located at the micropylar end.\n(c) The filiform apparatus is present at the micropylar tip of the synergids.\n(d) At maturity, the central cell contains two polar nuclei.\n(e) Each of the seven cells of the mature embryo sac contains exactly one nucleus.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c) and (d) are correct, giving 3. The antipodals lie at the chalazal end, not the micropylar end. The embryo sac is 7-celled but 8-nucleate because the central cell holds two polar nuclei, so not every cell is uninucleate.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section of a typical angiosperm anther looks four-sided (tetragonal), with one near-circular pollen sac at each corner. How many microsporangia does the whole anther have, and how many are in each lobe?","image":"","options":["2 in the anther; 1 in each lobe","4 in the anther; 2 in each lobe","4 in the anther; 4 in each lobe","8 in the anther; 4 in each lobe"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A typical anther is bilobed and tetrasporangiate. Its four microsporangia sit at the corners, two in each lobe. The trap is to treat each lobe as having four sporangia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In papaya, each plant bears either only male flowers or only female flowers. Which outcome does this condition ensure?","image":"","options":["It prevents autogamy but not geitonogamy","It prevents geitonogamy but not autogamy","It prevents both autogamy and geitonogamy","It prevents xenogamy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Papaya is dioecious: male and female flowers are on different plants. Pollen therefore cannot reach a stigma of the same flower or of the same plant, which blocks both autogamy and geitonogamy. Preventing only autogamy is true of monoecious plants such as castor and maize.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Wind- and water-pollinated flowers produce an enormous number of pollen grains compared with the number of ovules available.\nReason (R): In wind and water pollination, the contact of pollen grains with the stigma is a matter of chance.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Abiotic agents carry pollen without any direction, so most of it is lost. Producing huge amounts of pollen makes up for this uncertainty, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Parthenium (carrot grass), a common cause of pollen allergy in India, is believed to have entered the country:","image":"","options":["As an ornamental plant brought in by gardeners","As a contaminant with imported wheat","Through seeds carried by migratory birds","As a fodder crop introduced for cattle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Parthenium came into India as a contaminant with imported wheat. It has since become ubiquitous and causes pollen allergy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The integuments of an ovule completely enclose the nucellus without leaving any opening.\nStatement II: The cells of the nucellus contain abundant reserve food materials.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The integuments encircle the nucellus everywhere except at the tip, where they leave a small opening called the micropyle, so Statement I is incorrect. Nucellar cells are rich in reserve food, so Statement II is correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The proximal end of the filament of a stamen is attached to the:","image":"","options":["Thalamus or the petal of the flower","Stigma of the pistil","Ovary wall","Sepal only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The filament is the long, slender stalk of the stamen. Its proximal end is attached to the thalamus or to a petal, and its distal end bears the anther.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A breeder wants to cross a self-compatible variety with bisexual flowers (female parent) with another variety. She removes the anthers from fully opened flowers in which the anthers have already dehisced. She then bags the flowers, dusts pollen of the male parent on the receptive stigma and rebags them. Many progeny turn out to be products of self-pollination. The most likely error is that","image":"","options":["the flowers should not have been rebagged after pollination","emasculation should have been done in the bud stage, before the anthers dehisced","the bag should have been removed until the stigma became receptive","pollen should have been dusted before the stigma became receptive"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Emasculation must be done in the flower bud before anther dehiscence. Here the flower's own pollen had already been released and could reach its stigma before the anthers were removed. Bagging, pollinating at receptivity and rebagging were all done correctly.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a transverse section of a typical angiosperm anther, how many microsporangia are seen, and where are they located?","image":"","options":["Two, one in each lobe at the centre","Four, located at the corners, two in each lobe","Four, all clustered at the centre of the anther","Eight, four in each lobe"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A typical anther is bilobed and dithecous. In transverse section it appears tetragonal, with four microsporangia at its corners, two in each lobe. That is why it is called tetrasporangiate.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following produce albuminous seeds (endosperm persisting in the mature seed)?\nWheat, Pea, Maize, Groundnut, Barley, Castor, Coconut","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Wheat, maize, barley, castor and coconut retain endosperm in the mature seed, which makes 5. In pea and groundnut, the developing embryo consumes the endosperm completely, so their seeds are non-albuminous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A breeder wants to cross two varieties. The female parent bears bisexual flowers. Which sequence of steps is correct?","image":"","options":["Bag the open flower, remove anthers after they dehisce, dust desired pollen, rebag","Remove anthers from the flower bud before dehiscence, bag the flower, dust desired pollen once the stigma is receptive, rebag","Remove anthers from the flower bud before dehiscence, dust desired pollen immediately, then bag the flower","Bag the flower bud, dust desired pollen when the stigma is receptive; emasculation is not needed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"For bisexual flowers, the anthers must be removed from the bud before they dehisce. The flower is then bagged (usually with butter paper), pollinated with the chosen pollen when the stigma becomes receptive, and rebagged. Skipping emasculation is acceptable only when the female parent bears unisexual flowers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following plants produces both chasmogamous and cleistogamous flowers?","image":"","options":["Vallisneria","Commelina","Papaya","Maize"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Commelina, Viola (common pansy) and Oxalis bear both open (chasmogamous) and closed (cleistogamous) flowers. Vallisneria is water-pollinated, papaya is dioecious and maize is monoecious.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The nucleus of the functional megaspore divides to form a mature 8-nucleate embryo sac. How many successive mitotic nuclear divisions are needed?","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Eight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"One nucleus becomes 2, then 4, then 8 nuclei, so three successive free-nuclear mitoses are needed. 'Eight' is a trap because it confuses the number of nuclei with the number of divisions.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Pollination by water is rare in flowering plants and is limited to about 30 genera, mostly monocotyledons.\nStatement II: In Vallisneria, the female flowers remain submerged and the pollen grains are released inside the water.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In Vallisneria, the female flower reaches the water surface on a long stalk, and male flowers or pollen float on the surface. Submerged female flowers with pollen released inside water describe seagrasses, not Vallisneria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a mature seed of black pepper, which option correctly matches each structure with its origin and ploidy?","image":"","options":["Perisperm - nucellus - 2n; Seed coat - integuments - 2n; Pericarp - ovary wall - 2n","Perisperm - central cell - 3n; Seed coat - integuments - 2n; Pericarp - ovary wall - 2n","Perisperm - nucellus - 2n; Seed coat - nucellus - 2n; Pericarp - ovule wall - 3n","Perisperm - nucellus - n; Seed coat - integuments - 2n; Pericarp - ovary wall - n"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Perisperm is persistent nucellus, which is maternal diploid tissue. The seed coat comes from the integuments and the pericarp from the ovary wall, and both are also diploid. The triploid tissue is the endosperm, not the perisperm, which is the trap in option 2.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following conditions prevents both autogamy and geitonogamy?","image":"","options":["Monoecy, as seen in maize","Placement of anther and stigma at different positions in a bisexual flower","Dioecy, as seen in papaya","Release of pollen before the stigma of the same flower becomes receptive"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In dioecious plants such as papaya, each plant bears only male or only female flowers, so pollen from the same plant can never reach a stigma. Monoecy and the other two devices stop only autogamy; geitonogamy remains possible.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Geitonogamy is genetically similar to autogamy.\nReason (R): Geitonogamy requires a pollinating agent to transfer pollen between flowers.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both statements are true. Geitonogamy is genetically like autogamy because the pollen comes from the same plant, not because it needs a pollinating agent. The need for an agent is what makes it functionally cross-pollination, so R does not explain A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Among the animals that pollinate flowers, which group is the dominant biotic pollinating agent?","image":"","options":["Birds such as sunbirds","Bats","Bees","Arboreal rodents"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Insects are the main animal pollinators, and among insects bees dominate. Birds, bats and rodents also pollinate, but far less often.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following is the endosperm completely consumed by the developing embryo before the seed matures?","image":"","options":["Castor","Coconut","Groundnut","Maize"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In groundnut, as in pea and beans, the embryo uses up the endosperm before seed maturation. Castor, coconut and maize retain endosperm in the mature seed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about pollen viability is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The period of pollen viability depends to some extent on the prevailing temperature and humidity","Pollen grains of rice and wheat lose viability within about 30 minutes of release","Pollen grains of some members of Leguminoseae lose viability within 30 minutes of their release","Pollen grains of some members of Rosaceae and Solanaceae remain viable for months"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The very short 30-minute viability is described for cereals such as rice and wheat. Some members of Rosaceae, Leguminoseae and Solanaceae instead keep their pollen viable for months.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Syngamy\n(b) Triple fusion\n(c) Central cell after triple fusion\n(d) White kernel of coconut\nList-II\n(i) Primary endosperm cell\n(ii) Cellular endosperm\n(iii) Diploid zygote\n(iv) Triploid primary endosperm nucleus","image":"","options":["a-(iii), b-(iv), c-(i), d-(ii)","a-(iv), b-(iii), c-(i), d-(ii)","a-(iii), b-(iv), c-(ii), d-(i)","a-(i), b-(iv), c-(iii), d-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Syngamy produces the diploid zygote, and triple fusion produces the triploid PEN. The central cell then becomes the primary endosperm cell. The coconut kernel is cellular endosperm.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"An angiosperm has 2n = 16 chromosomes. What is the chromosome number in a nucellus cell, the functional megaspore, a synergid, and the primary endosperm nucleus formed after triple fusion, respectively?","image":"","options":["16, 8, 8, 24","8, 8, 8, 24","16, 16, 8, 24","16, 8, 8, 16"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The nucellus is sporophytic tissue, so it is diploid (16). Meiosis of the MMC gives a haploid functional megaspore (8), and mitosis keeps all embryo sac cells, including the synergids, haploid (8). Triple fusion joins two polar nuclei and one male gamete, giving a triploid PEN with 24 chromosomes. A common trap is to treat the nucellus as haploid or to count only the two polar nuclei.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following have albuminous seeds?\nWheat, pea, maize, groundnut, castor, barley, beans","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Wheat, maize, castor and barley retain part of the endosperm in the mature seed. In pea, groundnut and beans, the endosperm is completely consumed during embryo development.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly shows the sequence of stages in the development of a dicotyledonous embryo?","image":"","options":["Zygote → globular → proembryo → heart-shaped → mature embryo","Zygote → heart-shaped → globular → proembryo → mature embryo","Zygote → proembryo → globular → heart-shaped → mature embryo","Zygote → proembryo → heart-shaped → globular → mature embryo"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The zygote first forms the proembryo. This then passes through the globular stage and the heart-shaped stage before becoming the mature embryo. The usual trap is placing the heart-shaped stage before the globular stage.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which wall layer of a microsporangium nourishes the developing pollen grains?","image":"","options":["Epidermis","Endothecium","Middle layers","Tapetum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The tapetum is the innermost wall layer of the microsporangium and it nourishes the developing pollen grains. The outer three layers protect the microsporangium and help in anther dehiscence.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many successive mitotic nuclear divisions of the functional megaspore nucleus are needed to reach the 8-nucleate stage of the embryo sac?","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Eight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The divisions go 1 → 2 → 4 → 8 nuclei, so three sequential free-nuclear mitoses are needed. 'Eight' confuses the number of nuclei with the number of divisions.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following produce albuminous seeds?\nWheat, Pea, Castor, Groundnut, Maize, Barley, Beans","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Two"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Wheat, castor, maize and barley keep part of their endosperm in the mature seed, so they are albuminous. In pea, groundnut and beans, the developing embryo uses up the endosperm completely, so these seeds are non-albuminous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are diploid in a typical angiosperm?\nNucellus cell, megaspore mother cell, functional megaspore, synergid, zygote, primary endosperm nucleus, integument cell, egg cell","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The nucellus, MMC, integument cell and zygote are diploid. The functional megaspore, synergid and egg are haploid, and the PEN is triploid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about outbreeding devices is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Monoecious plants such as castor and maize prevent autogamy but not geitonogamy","Dioecy, as in papaya, prevents both autogamy and geitonogamy","Self-incompatibility can block fertilisation by pollen from other flowers of the same plant","Geitonogamy is genetically equivalent to xenogamy because it requires a pollinating agent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Geitonogamy is functionally like cross-pollination because it needs an agent. Genetically, however, it resembles autogamy, since the pollen comes from the same plant. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In most angiosperms, the zygote divides only after a certain amount of endosperm has formed.\nReason (R): Endosperm cells are filled with reserve food materials that nourish the developing embryo.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Endosperm development precedes embryo development so that the embryo is assured of nutrition. The endosperm supplies this nutrition through its stored food, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The exine of a pollen grain is made of sporopollenin, which is absent at the germ pores.\nStatement II: The intine is a thin, continuous layer made of cellulose and pectin.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The exine is made of sporopollenin, except at the germ pores where it is absent. The intine is the thin, continuous inner wall made of cellulose and pectin, so both statements are correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are triploid?\nPrimary endosperm nucleus; nucellus cell; perisperm cell of black pepper; synergid; cell of the white kernel of coconut; antipodal cell; zygote","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only the primary endosperm nucleus and the cellular endosperm (coconut kernel) are triploid, since both result from triple fusion. Nucellus, perisperm (residual nucellus) and the zygote are diploid, while synergids and antipodals are haploid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Persistent nucellus (perisperm)\n(B) Fruit formed without fertilisation\n(C) Thalamus contributes to fruit\n(D) Nucellar embryos in a seed\nList-II\n(I) Strawberry\n(II) Citrus\n(III) Black pepper\n(IV) Banana\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Black pepper (like beet) has perisperm, and banana is parthenocarpic. Strawberry is a false fruit because its thalamus contributes to the fruit, and Citrus shows polyembryony through nucellar embryos.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the seeds of black pepper and beet, some of the nucellus persists after the seed matures. What is this residual tissue called?","image":"","options":["Pericarp","Perisperm","Endosperm","Scutellum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Persistent nucellus in a mature seed is called perisperm. Pericarp is the fruit wall formed from the ovary wall, so the similar-sounding name is the trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A seed of a mango variety contains one embryo formed from the zygote and several embryos formed from nucellar cells. Which statement about these embryos is correct?","image":"","options":["All the embryos are genetically identical to one another because they share the same ovule","The nucellar embryos are haploid, while the zygotic embryo is diploid","The nucellar embryos are clones of the mother plant, whereas the zygotic embryo may differ genetically from it","The zygotic embryo is a clone of the mother plant, whereas the nucellar embryos show new gene combinations"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Nucellar embryos come from diploid maternal cells without meiosis or fertilisation, so they are maternal clones. The zygotic embryo comes from meiosis plus fusion with a male gamete, so it can carry new combinations. Sharing an ovule does not make the embryos genetically identical.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical angiosperm ovule, the region where the body of the ovule fuses with the funicle is called the:","image":"","options":["Micropyle","Chalaza","Hilum","Nucellus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The hilum is the junction between the ovule and its stalk, the funicle. The micropyle is the opening at the tip of the integuments, and the chalaza is the basal part of the ovule opposite the micropyle.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The NCERT diagram of a typical angiosperm ovule shows an anatropous ovule, in which the body of the ovule is inverted on its stalk. Which of the following correctly describes the arrangement of its parts?","image":"","options":["The micropyle lies close to the funicle and hilum, while the chalaza lies at the opposite end","The micropyle lies at the end farthest from the funicle, in a straight line with the funicle and chalaza","The chalaza lies next to the hilum, and the micropyle points away from the placenta","The funicle is attached directly at the micropyle, and the hilum lies at the chalazal end"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Because the anatropous ovule is turned upside down, its micropyle comes to lie near the funicle and hilum, with the chalaza at the far end. A straight arrangement of funicle, chalaza and micropyle describes an erect (orthotropous) ovule instead.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Flowering plants have evolved many devices that discourage self-pollination and encourage cross-pollination. According to NCERT, the main problem with continued self-pollination is that it leads to","image":"","options":["parthenocarpy","inbreeding depression","polyembryony","apomixis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Repeated self-pollination causes inbreeding depression, and outbreeding devices evolved to avoid it. The other options are unrelated reproductive phenomena.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about autogamy in chasmogamous (open) flowers is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Pollen release and stigma receptivity must be synchronised","The anthers and stigma should lie close to each other","Complete autogamy is very common in such flowers","Pollen is transferred to the stigma of the same flower"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In normal flowers that open and expose their anthers and stigma, complete autogamy is rather rare. It needs both synchrony of pollen release with stigma receptivity and close placement of anthers and stigma.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Endothecium\nB. Tapetum\nC. Sporogenous tissue\nD. Filament\nList-II\nI. Proximal end attached to the thalamus or petal\nII. Outer wall layer that protects and helps in anther dehiscence\nIII. Cells with dense cytoplasm, often more than one nucleus; nourish pollen\nIV. Compact homogenous cells that undergo meiosis to form microspore tetrads\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The endothecium is one of the outer protective layers that aids dehiscence. The tapetum has dense, often multinucleate cells that nourish the pollen. Sporogenous cells undergo meiosis to form microspore tetrads, and the filament is attached to the thalamus or petal. The key trap is confusing the endothecium with the tapetum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about pollinating agents is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Reptiles such as the gecko lizard and garden lizard have been reported as pollinators in some species","Flowers pollinated by flies and beetles secrete foul odours to attract them","An insect that consumes nectar without touching the anthers and stigma is called a nectar robber","A majority of flowering plants use abiotic agents, with wind being the most common of all agents"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Most flowering plants use animals (biotic agents), and only a small proportion use wind or water. Wind is the most common agent only among abiotic pollinations. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Dioecy, as seen in papaya, prevents both autogamy and geitonogamy.\nStatement II: Self-incompatibility prevents fertilisation by pollen from the same flower only, but not by pollen from other flowers of the same plant.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A dioecious plant is either male or female, so neither autogamy nor geitonogamy is possible. Self-incompatibility, however, blocks self-pollen from the same flower and from other flowers of the same plant, so Statement II is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following structures associated with an angiosperm ovule differs in ploidy from the other three?","image":"","options":["Nucellus","Integument","Funicle","Synergid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The nucellus, integuments and funicle are sporophytic tissues, so they are diploid. A synergid belongs to the embryo sac (female gametophyte), which develops from a meiotically formed megaspore, so it is haploid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a developing anther, what is the ploidy of a pollen mother cell (PMC) and of a cell of the microspore tetrad, respectively?","image":"","options":["2n and n","n and n","2n and 2n","n and 2n"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each PMC comes from the diploid sporogenous tissue and undergoes meiosis. Its four products, the cells of the microspore tetrad, are therefore haploid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If apomictic genes are transferred into a hybrid variety, farmers could sow seeds saved from that hybrid year after year without the progeny losing hybrid characters.\nStatement II: Apomixis is a form of sexual reproduction, because the seeds it produces arise after normal meiosis and fertilisation.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Apomictic seeds form without fertilisation, for example from a diploid egg formed without reduction division or from nucellar cells. Hybrid characters therefore do not segregate, and Statement I is correct. Apomixis is asexual reproduction that only mimics sexual reproduction, so Statement II is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Pollen grains are well preserved as fossils.\nReason (R): The exine of pollen grains contains sporopollenin, which resists high temperature, strong acids, alkalis and enzymatic degradation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Sporopollenin is extremely resistant, and no enzyme degrading it is known. This is exactly why pollen grains survive as fossils, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Papaver\nB. Michelia\nC. Papaya\nD. Wheat\nList-II\nI. Multicarpellary, apocarpous gynoecium\nII. Ovary containing a single ovule\nIII. Multicarpellary, syncarpous pistil\nIV. Ovary containing many ovules\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","image":"","options":["A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Papaver has fused carpels (syncarpous), while Michelia has free carpels (apocarpous). Papaya ovaries bear many ovules, whereas wheat, like paddy and mango, has a single ovule per ovary. The usual trap is swapping the syncarpous and apocarpous examples.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the Yucca–moth relationship:\n(i) The moth deposits its eggs in the locule of the ovary.\n(ii) The moth larvae come out of the eggs as the seeds start developing.\n(iii) Yucca can complete its life cycle without the moth by switching to wind pollination.\n(iv) The floral reward offered to the moth is mainly nectar.\nWhich of the above are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii) only","(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(ii) and (iv) only","(i) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The moth lays its eggs in the ovary's locule and pollinates the flower, and the larvae emerge as the seeds develop. Neither species can complete its life cycle without the other, so (iii) is wrong. The reward is a site for the moth's eggs, not nectar, so (iv) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for a mature angiosperm embryo sac:\nList-I\n(A) Egg apparatus\n(B) Antipodals\n(C) Central cell\n(D) Filiform apparatus\nList-II\n(I) Three cells at the chalazal end\n(II) Two synergids and one egg cell at the micropylar end\n(III) Guides pollen tube entry\n(IV) Large cell containing two polar nuclei\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The egg apparatus is the micropylar group of two synergids and one egg, and the antipodals are three chalazal cells. The central cell holds the two polar nuclei, and the filiform apparatus of the synergids guides the pollen tube.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The pistil can distinguish compatible pollen from incompatible pollen.\n(ii) Recognition of pollen is mediated by chemical components of the pollen interacting with those of the pistil.\n(iii) Incompatible pollen can be rejected only at the stigma; pollen tube growth in the style is never inhibited.\n(iv) A compatible pollen grain produces its pollen tube through one of the germ pores.\n(v) Pollen of pea dusted on a slide with about 10 per cent sugar solution can show pollen tubes after about 15–30 minutes.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iv) and (v) are correct, so the count is four. Statement (iii) is wrong because the pistil rejects wrong pollen either by preventing germination on the stigma or by inhibiting pollen tube growth in the style.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about a typical angiosperm embryo sac is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The three mitotic divisions forming it are free nuclear","The two polar nuclei lie below the egg apparatus in the large central cell","The filiform apparatus is present at the chalazal tips of the antipodal cells","At maturity it is 8-nucleate but 7-celled"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The filiform apparatus is a cellular thickening at the micropylar tip of the synergids, where it guides the pollen tube. Antipodals lie at the chalazal end and have no such structure.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"As a seed matures, its water content falls. What is the approximate moisture content of a mature, relatively dry seed?","image":"","options":["1-2 per cent by mass","10-15 per cent by mass","40-50 per cent by mass","70-80 per cent by mass"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mature seeds dry to about 10-15 per cent moisture by mass. This dehydration slows the metabolic activity of the embryo and allows seeds to be stored.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the anther and microsporangium are correct?\n(i) The tapetum is the innermost wall layer of the microsporangium.\n(ii) Microsporangia extend longitudinally all through the length of the anther.\n(iii) In transverse section, a typical microsporangium appears nearly circular.\n(iv) The sporogenous tissue of a young anther consists of loosely arranged, heterogenous cells.\n(v) Tapetal cells are generally uninucleate with sparse cytoplasm.\n(vi) The endothecium is the outermost wall layer of the microsporangium.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only (i), (ii) and (iii) are correct. Sporogenous cells are compact and homogenous, tapetal cells have dense cytoplasm and usually more than one nucleus, and the epidermis, not the endothecium, is the outermost layer.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In maize, the silky threads that hang out of a corn cob and wave in the wind are actually the:","image":"","options":["Filaments of stamens","Stigma and style","Bracts of the inflorescence","Elongated anthers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The silky threads of the corn cob are stigmas and styles. They wave in the wind to trap airborne pollen. They are not stamens, because the cob carries the female flowers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Self-incompatibility\n(B) Dioecy\n(C) Emasculation\n(D) Non-synchrony of pollen release and stigma receptivity\nList-II\n(I) Removal of anthers from a flower bud with forceps\n(II) Pollen shed before the stigma is receptive, or the stigma receptive much before pollen release\n(III) Genetic mechanism that inhibits germination or tube growth of self-pollen in the pistil\n(IV) Papaya\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Self-incompatibility is a genetic block on self-pollen. Papaya is dioecious. Emasculation is the removal of anthers. Non-synchrony means a mismatch between pollen release and stigma receptivity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Cells of the tapetum have dense cytoplasm and generally have more than one nucleus. Which process most likely makes a tapetal cell binucleate?","image":"","options":["Fusion of two microspores with the tapetal cell","Nuclear division that is not followed by cytokinesis","Meiosis of the tapetal cell producing two haploid nuclei inside it","Entry of a nucleus from the endothecium into the tapetal cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"If the nucleus divides but the cytoplasm does not, the cell ends up with two nuclei inside one cell. Tapetal cells do not take part in meiosis, which happens only in the sporogenous cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Choose the correct ploidy, in order, for: nucellus cell, megaspore mother cell, functional megaspore and female gametophyte cell.","image":"","options":["2n, n, n, n","2n, 2n, n, n","n, 2n, n, 2n","2n, 2n, 2n, n"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The nucellus and the MMC are diploid. Meiosis of the MMC gives haploid megaspores, and the embryo sac formed from the functional megaspore by mitosis stays haploid. The common trap is marking the MMC as haploid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Research labs are trying to transfer apomictic genes into hybrid crop varieties. Which reason best explains why apomictic hybrids would benefit farmers?","image":"","options":["Apomictic embryos form without meiosis and fertilisation, so progeny keep the hybrid genotype and the farmer can reuse saved seed every year","Apomixis increases genetic recombination, so each new generation is more vigorous than the original hybrid","Apomixis induces parthenocarpy, so hybrid fruits become seedless and fetch a better price","Apomixis makes hybrids self-incompatible, preventing contamination by unwanted pollen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Without meiosis and fertilisation, the hybrid's characters do not segregate in the progeny. This saves the farmer the cost of buying fresh hybrid seed each year. Apomixis does not increase recombination, and it does not produce seedless fruits.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about megasporogenesis and the embryo sac are correct?\n(a) The megaspore mother cell differentiates in the chalazal region of the nucellus.\n(b) The megaspore mother cell undergoes meiosis to form four megaspores.\n(c) In a majority of flowering plants, only one megaspore is functional.\n(d) Cell walls are laid down only after the 8-nucleate stage of the embryo sac.\n(e) The polar nuclei are situated above the egg apparatus, near the micropyle.\n(f) The antipodals lie at the chalazal end of the embryo sac.","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements b, c, d and f are correct. The MMC actually differentiates in the micropylar region of the nucellus, so (a) is wrong. The polar nuclei lie below the egg apparatus in the central cell, so (e) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Pollen grains are well preserved as fossils.\nReason (R): The exine of pollen grains is made of cellulose and pectin.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Pollen fossilises well because its exine contains sporopollenin, which withstands heat, strong acids and alkalis. Cellulose and pectin make up the intine, not the exine, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The term double fertilisation refers to the two fusion events, syngamy and triple fusion, that occur within the same embryo sac.\nStatement II: In the most common type of endosperm development, each nuclear division of the PEN is immediately followed by cell wall formation.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I correctly defines double fertilisation. In the commonest type of endosperm development, however, the PEN first undergoes free-nuclear divisions, and cell walls form only later.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Panchanan Maheshwari is best known for which contribution to plant science?","image":"","options":["Discovering double fertilisation in angiosperms","Popularising the use of embryological characters in taxonomy","Discovering the chemical nature of sporopollenin","Proposing the five-kingdom classification"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Maheshwari worked on embryology and popularised the use of embryological characters in taxonomy. He also made the Department of Botany, University of Delhi, a centre for embryology and tissue culture research.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In most flowering plants, three of the four megaspores formed by meiosis of the MMC degenerate.\nStatement II: In a mature embryo sac, the two polar nuclei lie just above the antipodals at the chalazal end, outside the central cell.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct because only one megaspore is functional. Statement II is incorrect because the polar nuclei sit below the egg apparatus inside the large central cell, not outside it at the chalazal end.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"After reaching the ovary, a compatible pollen tube enters the ovule through the ______ and then enters ______.","image":"","options":["chalaza; the egg cell directly","micropyle; one of the synergids through the filiform apparatus","integument; the central cell","hilum; one of the antipodal cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The pollen tube enters the ovule through the micropyle. It then enters one of the synergids, guided by the filiform apparatus at the synergid's micropylar tip. It does not enter the egg, the central cell or the antipodals directly.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Flowering plants have evolved many outbreeding devices. Their main purpose is to avoid which harmful consequence of continued self-pollination?","image":"","options":["Parthenocarpy","Inbreeding depression","Polyembryony","Apomixis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Most angiosperms bear hermaphrodite flowers, and continued self-pollination results in inbreeding depression. Devices that discourage selfing and favour cross-pollination counter this. The other options are unrelated reproductive phenomena.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (part of pistil) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\nA. Stigma\nB. Style\nC. Ovary\nD. Placenta\nList-II:\n(i) Basal bulged part of the pistil\n(ii) Landing platform for pollen grains\n(iii) Elongated slender part beneath the stigma\n(iv) Structure inside the ovarian cavity from which ovules arise\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The stigma receives pollen, the style is the slender stalk below it, and the ovary is the swollen base. The placenta lies in the locule and bears the ovules.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures are present in a typical dicotyledonous embryo?\nPlumule, radicle, coleoptile, root cap, scutellum, hypocotyl, coleorrhiza","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A typical dicot embryo has a plumule, radicle, root cap and hypocotyl, which makes four. The coleoptile, scutellum and coleorrhiza are features of the grass (monocot) embryo.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cleistogamous flowers produce assured seed-set even in the absence of pollinators.\nReason (R): In cleistogamous flowers, the anthers dehisce inside the closed bud and the pollen reaches the stigma, which lies close to the anthers.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Cleistogamous flowers never open, and their anthers and stigma lie close together. Pollen released inside the bud therefore pollinates the stigma without any external agent, which is exactly why seed-set is assured.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, apomixis has evolved in some species of which two plant groups?","image":"","options":["Rosaceae and Solanaceae","Leguminosae and orchids","Asteraceae and grasses","Brassicaceae and Liliaceae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT names some species of Asteraceae and grasses as apomictic. Rosaceae, Leguminosae and Solanaceae are mentioned in the chapter only for long pollen viability.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The filiform apparatus, which guides the pollen tube, is a special cellular thickening found at the micropylar tip of the:","image":"","options":["Egg cell","Antipodal cells","Synergids","Central cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Synergids carry the filiform apparatus at their micropylar tip, and it directs the pollen tube into a synergid. The egg is the other member of the egg apparatus but does not have this structure.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the embryo of a member of the grass family, the single cotyledon is called the","image":"","options":["coleoptile","coleorrhiza","plumule","scutellum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In grasses, the single cotyledon is called the scutellum, and it lies to one side of the embryonal axis. The coleoptile and coleorrhiza are sheaths, not cotyledons.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a maize plant, an insect carries pollen from a male flower to a female flower of the same plant, and seeds are formed. Which option correctly identifies the pollination type and its genetic outcome?","image":"","options":["Xenogamy; the progeny carry genes from two different parents","Autogamy; it occurs within a single bisexual flower","Geitonogamy; genetically equivalent to self-pollination though it uses an agent","Cleistogamy; pollination took place inside an unopened flower"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The pollen moved between two flowers of the same plant, which is geitonogamy. Since all the pollen comes from one plant, it is genetically like selfing. Monoecy prevents autogamy but cannot prevent geitonogamy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Vallisneria\n(B) Zostera\n(C) Yucca\n(D) Viola\nList-II\n(I) Female flowers stay submerged and pollen is released inside water\n(II) Produces both chasmogamous and cleistogamous flowers\n(III) Female flower reaches the water surface on a long stalk\n(IV) Cannot complete its life cycle without a particular moth species\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In Vallisneria, pollination happens at the water surface, while in seagrasses like Zostera it happens under water. Yucca and its moth depend on each other, and Viola bears both types of flowers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a young anther, a group of compactly arranged, homogenous cells occupies the centre of each microsporangium. This group of cells is called the:","image":"","options":["Tapetum","Endothecium","Sporogenous tissue","Connective tissue"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The sporogenous tissue fills the centre of each microsporangium in a young anther. Its cells later undergo meiosis to form microspore tetrads.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical dicot embryo, the cylindrical portion of the embryonal axis below the level of the cotyledons ends at its lower end in the","image":"","options":["radicle","plumule","epicotyl","coleoptile"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The portion below the cotyledons is the hypocotyl, and it terminates in the radicle (root tip), which is covered by a root cap. The plumule lies at the tip of the epicotyl, above the cotyledons.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about pollen-pistil interaction is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The pollen tube emerges through one of the germ pores of the pollen grain","In species shedding 2-celled pollen, the generative cell divides to form two male gametes while the pollen tube grows in the stigma","The pollen tube enters the ovule through the micropyle and then enters a synergid","Pollen-pistil interaction includes all events up to the fusion of a male gamete with the egg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Pollen-pistil interaction spans the events from pollen landing on the stigma to the pollen tube entering the ovule or embryo sac. Syngamy is part of double fertilisation, not of this interaction. The other three statements match NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Triple fusion in angiosperms involves the fusion of three haploid nuclei, namely one male gamete and the two polar nuclei.\nStatement II: After entering the ovule, the pollen tube releases its two male gametes directly into the cytoplasm of the egg cell.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In triple fusion, one male gamete fuses with the two polar nuclei of the central cell to form the triploid PEN. The pollen tube enters a synergid and discharges both male gametes into the synergid's cytoplasm, not the egg's. One gamete then moves to the egg and the other to the central cell.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A plant belongs to the majority group of angiosperms in terms of the stage at which pollen is shed. For a pollen grain of this plant at the time of shedding, which option correctly states the number of male gametes present and how they are eventually formed?","image":"","options":["Two male gametes, formed by meiosis of the generative cell before shedding","No male gametes; the vegetative cell divides mitotically inside the pollen tube to form them","No male gametes; after compatible pollination, the generative cell divides mitotically during pollen tube growth in the stigma","One male gamete; the second forms from the generative cell only after entry into the synergid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Over 60% of angiosperms shed pollen at the 2-celled stage, which has a vegetative cell and a generative cell but no male gametes yet. The generative cell divides mitotically to form the two male gametes while the pollen tube grows in the stigma; the vegetative cell does not form gametes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about cleistogamous flowers are correct?\n(i) Their anthers dehisce while the flower is still a bud.\n(ii) They are invariably autogamous.\n(iii) They depend on insects to achieve pollination.\n(iv) They ensure seed-set even when pollinators are absent.\n(v) Cross-pollen can frequently land on their stigma.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iv) are correct. Because cleistogamous flowers never open, they do not need pollinators, and no cross-pollen can reach the stigma, so (iii) and (v) are wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about a typical mature angiosperm embryo sac is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The synergids bear a filiform apparatus at their micropylar tip","The two polar nuclei lie at the micropylar end, above the egg apparatus","The mitotic divisions that form the embryo sac are strictly free nuclear","Three antipodal cells are located at the chalazal end"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The polar nuclei lie in the large central cell, below the egg apparatus, not above it at the micropylar end. All the other statements match NCERT's description of the 7-celled, 8-nucleate embryo sac.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Pollen grains of many species can be stored for years and used in crop breeding programmes as pollen banks. The storage is done in:","image":"","options":["Dry ice at $-78^{\\circ}C$","A deep freezer at $-20^{\\circ}C$","Liquid oxygen at $-183^{\\circ}C$","Liquid nitrogen at $-196^{\\circ}C$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that pollen grains of a large number of species can be stored for years in liquid nitrogen at $-196^{\\circ}C$. Pollen banks work like seed banks.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about pollen grains are correct?\n(i) The vegetative cell is larger than the generative cell and has abundant food reserve.\n(ii) The generative cell is spindle shaped with dense cytoplasm and a nucleus.\n(iii) In over 60 per cent of angiosperms, pollen grains are shed at the 3-celled stage.\n(iv) The two male gametes arise from the generative cell by meiosis.\n(v) The intine is a thin and continuous layer.\n(vi) Germ pores are regions of the intine where cellulose is absent.","image":"","options":["2","4","5","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (v) are correct, so the count is 3. In over 60% of angiosperms pollen is shed at the 2-celled stage, and the generative cell divides mitotically, not meiotically. Germ pores are regions of the exine that lack sporopollenin.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In some apomictic species, a diploid egg cell is formed without reduction division and develops into an embryo without fertilisation.\nStatement II: When seeds harvested from a non-apomictic hybrid crop are sown, the progeny retain all the hybrid characters without segregation.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A diploid, unreduced egg developing without fertilisation is one route to apomictic seed, so Statement I is correct. Seeds collected from ordinary hybrids give progeny that segregate, so hybrid seed must be produced every year; Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Apomixis\n(B) Parthenocarpy\n(C) Polyembryony\n(D) Perisperm\nList-II\n(I) More than one embryo in a seed\n(II) Residual persistent nucellus in a seed\n(III) Seed formation without fertilisation\n(IV) Fruit formation without fertilisation\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Apomixis gives seeds without fertilisation, and parthenocarpy gives fruits without fertilisation (e.g., banana). Polyembryony means many embryos in one seed. Perisperm is persistent nucellus, as in black pepper and beet. The common trap is swapping apomixis with parthenocarpy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Cleistogamous flowers are best described as flowers that:","image":"","options":["open widely and expose their anthers and stigma","do not open at all","carry only stamens and no pistil","open only at night for moth pollination"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cleistogamous flowers stay closed. Their anthers and stigma lie close together inside the flower. Flowers that open and expose their anthers and stigma are chasmogamous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A typical mature angiosperm embryo sac has eight nuclei but only seven cells.\nReason (R): The two polar nuclei are not separated by cell walls and lie together in the single large central cell.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The embryo sac has 3 egg-apparatus cells, 3 antipodals and 1 central cell, which makes seven cells. The central cell holds two polar nuclei, which brings the nucleus count to eight. R therefore directly explains why the number of nuclei exceeds the number of cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Lupinus arcticus\n(B) Phoenix dactylifera\n(C) Banana\n(D) Orobanche\nList-II\n(I) Viable seed about 2000 years old, found near the Dead Sea\n(II) Parasitic plant whose fruits contain a very large number of seeds\n(III) Seed that germinated after about 10,000 years of dormancy in the Arctic Tundra\n(IV) Fruit that develops without fertilisation\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The lupine seed from the Arctic Tundra germinated after about 10,000 years. The date palm seed from King Herod's palace was about 2000 years old. Banana is parthenocarpic, and Orobanche is a parasite that produces fruits with thousands of seeds.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Emasculation\n(b) Bagging\n(c) Self-incompatibility\n(d) Filiform apparatus\nList-II\n(i) Guides the pollen tube into the synergid\n(ii) Removal of anthers from a bud before dehiscence\n(iii) Genetic inhibition of germination or tube growth of self-pollen\n(iv) Covering a flower to keep unwanted pollen off the stigma","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Emasculation removes anthers from the bud with forceps before they dehisce. Bagging covers the flower to prevent stigma contamination. Self-incompatibility is a genetic block on self-pollen, and the filiform apparatus of synergids directs pollen tube entry.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Filament\n(b) Tapetum\n(c) Endothecium\n(d) Sporogenous tissue\nList-II\n(i) Undergoes meiosis to form microspore tetrads\n(ii) Protection and help in anther dehiscence\n(iii) Nourishment of developing pollen grains\n(iv) Slender stalk bearing the anther\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The filament is the stalk that bears the anther, and the tapetum nourishes the developing pollen. The endothecium is one of the outer layers that protect and aid dehiscence, and sporogenous cells undergo meiosis to form microspore tetrads.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Autogamy\n(B) Geitonogamy\n(C) Xenogamy\n(D) Cleistogamy\nList-II\n(I) Pollen reaches the stigma of a flower on a different plant\n(II) Flowers never open, so pollination occurs inside the bud\n(III) Pollen reaches the stigma of the same flower\n(IV) Pollen reaches the stigma of another flower on the same plant","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Autogamy is within the same flower, geitonogamy is between flowers of the same plant, and xenogamy is between plants. Cleistogamy refers to flowers that never open and so self-pollinate inside the bud.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The primary endosperm nucleus (PEN) formed by triple fusion is:","image":"","options":["Haploid, formed by fusion of a male gamete with one polar nucleus","Diploid, formed by fusion of the two polar nuclei only","Triploid, formed by fusion of a male gamete with the two polar nuclei","Triploid, formed by fusion of a male gamete with the egg and a synergid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Triple fusion joins three haploid nuclei: one male gamete and the two polar nuclei of the central cell. The result is a triploid PEN.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following features are typical of wind-pollinated flowers?\nLight, non-sticky pollen; large, often feathery stigma; abundant nectar; single ovule in each ovary; bright colourful petals; well-exposed stamens; numerous flowers packed into an inflorescence; sticky pollen","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Five features apply: light non-sticky pollen, feathery stigma, single ovule, exposed stamens and numerous flowers packed together. Nectar, bright colours and sticky pollen belong to animal-pollinated flowers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The residual, persistent nucellus found in some mature seeds is called perisperm. In which of the following seeds is perisperm present?","image":"","options":["Groundnut","Black pepper","Pea","Castor"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Perisperm is leftover nucellus that persists in seeds such as black pepper and beet. Castor retains endosperm, not nucellus, while pea and groundnut are non-albuminous seeds.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Perisperm\nB. Parthenocarpic fruit\nC. False fruit\nD. Nucellar polyembryony\nList-II\n(i) Citrus\n(ii) Black pepper\n(iii) Strawberry\n(iv) Banana\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-i, B-iv, C-iii, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Black pepper retains a persistent nucellus (perisperm), and banana develops fruit without fertilisation. In strawberry the thalamus contributes to the fruit, and Citrus shows embryos arising from nucellar cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (parts of a grass embryo) with List-II (their descriptions) and choose the correct option.\nList-I\nA. Scutellum\nB. Coleoptile\nC. Coleorrhiza\nD. Epicotyl\nList-II\n(i) Undifferentiated sheath enclosing the radicle and root cap\n(ii) Hollow foliar structure enclosing the shoot apex and a few leaf primordia\n(iii) Single cotyledon placed laterally on the embryonal axis\n(iv) Portion of the embryonal axis above the level of attachment of the scutellum","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In grasses, the cotyledon is the scutellum and lies to one side of the embryonal axis. The coleoptile sheathes the shoot apex and leaf primordia, while the coleorrhiza sheathes the radicle and root cap. The epicotyl is the part of the axis above the scutellum's attachment. Students often swap coleoptile (shoot) and coleorrhiza (root).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which type of pollination is the only one that brings genetically different pollen grains to the stigma?","image":"","options":["Autogamy","Geitonogamy","Cleistogamy","Xenogamy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Only xenogamy transfers pollen from a different plant, so only it delivers genetically different pollen. In geitonogamy the pollen comes from the same plant, so it is genetically like self-pollen.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The exine of a pollen grain is made of sporopollenin, which only a few specific enzymes can degrade.\nStatement II: Germ pores are regions on the exine where sporopollenin is absent.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Sporopollenin withstands high temperatures, strong acids and alkali, and no enzyme that degrades it is known so far. Statement I is therefore wrong, while germ pores are indeed the sporopollenin-free apertures of the exine.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Apomixis, seen in a few flowering plants, is best described as:","image":"","options":["Formation of fruits without fertilisation","Formation of seeds without fertilisation","Formation of more than one embryo in a seed after double fertilisation","Fusion of a male gamete with two polar nuclei"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Apomixis is the production of seeds without fertilisation. It is a form of asexual reproduction that mimics sexual reproduction. Fruit formation without fertilisation is a different phenomenon called parthenocarpy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The pistil of Papaver is best described as:","image":"","options":["Monocarpellary","Multicarpellary and apocarpous","Multicarpellary and syncarpous","Monocarpellary and syncarpous"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Papaver has more than one carpel, and the carpels are fused together, so its pistil is multicarpellary and syncarpous. Michelia is the example of a multicarpellary gynoecium with free (apocarpous) pistils.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following floral conditions prevents both autogamy and geitonogamy?","image":"","options":["Monoecy, as seen in castor and maize","Dioecy, as seen in papaya","Non-synchronised pollen release and stigma receptivity","Placement of anther and stigma at different positions in the same flower"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a dioecious plant like papaya, each plant bears either male or female flowers, so neither the same flower nor another flower of the same plant can supply pollen. Monoecy and the other two devices stop only autogamy, so geitonogamy can still occur.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The filiform apparatus consists of special cellular thickenings at the micropylar tip of the synergids and helps guide the pollen tube into a synergid.\nStatement II: The egg apparatus of a typical embryo sac consists of one egg cell and two antipodal cells.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The filiform apparatus belongs to the synergids and guides pollen tube entry, so Statement I is correct. The egg apparatus is made of two synergids and one egg. The antipodals form a separate group of three cells at the chalazal end, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (part of the embryo) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\nA. Scutellum\nB. Coleorrhiza\nC. Coleoptile\nD. Hypocotyl\nList-II:\nI. Hollow foliar structure enclosing the shoot apex and leaf primordia\nII. Single lateral cotyledon of the grass embryo\nIII. Portion of the embryonal axis below the cotyledons, ending in the radicle\nIV. Undifferentiated sheath enclosing the radicle and root cap","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In grasses the cotyledon is the scutellum. The coleorrhiza sheaths the radicle and root cap, the coleoptile encloses the shoot apex, and the hypocotyl lies below the cotyledons and ends in the radicle. A common trap is swapping coleoptile (shoot) with coleorrhiza (root).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The hard outer wall layer (exine) of a pollen grain is made up mainly of:","image":"","options":["Cellulose and pectin","Sporopollenin","Lignin and suberin","Callose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The exine is made of sporopollenin, one of the most resistant organic materials known. Cellulose and pectin make up the inner wall, the intine, which is the common trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In an angiosperm ovule, at which end of the embryo sac does the embryo develop after fertilisation?","image":"","options":["Chalazal end, where the antipodals are located","Micropylar end, where the zygote is situated","Centre of the embryo sac, where the central cell lies","Hilum region, where the funicle joins the ovule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The zygote forms from the egg cell, which is part of the egg apparatus at the micropylar end. So the embryo develops at the micropylar end. The chalazal end holds the antipodals, not the zygote.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (structure in seed or fruit) with List-II (part from which it develops).\nList-I:\nA. Seed coat\nB. Pericarp\nC. Perisperm\nD. Endosperm\nList-II:\nI. Residual nucellus\nII. Primary endosperm cell\nIII. Integuments\nIV. Ovary wall","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Integuments harden into the seed coat, and the ovary wall becomes the pericarp. Persistent nucellus is the perisperm (as in black pepper and beet), while the endosperm develops from the triploid primary endosperm cell.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Parthenium (carrot grass) is now widespread in India and causes pollen allergy. According to NCERT, it entered India as a contaminant of:","image":"","options":["Imported rice","Imported cotton seeds","Imported wheat","Imported soybean"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Parthenium came into India as a contaminant of imported wheat. It has since become ubiquitous, and its pollen causes allergies.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants have seeds in which the endosperm is completely consumed by the developing embryo before seed maturation?\nPea, Castor, Groundnut, Coconut, Beans, Wheat","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In pea, groundnut and beans, the endosperm is fully used up before the seed matures, so these seeds are non-albuminous. Castor, coconut and wheat retain endosperm in the mature seed, which is used up during germination. The trap is counting cereals or castor as non-albuminous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The cavity inside the ovary, in which the placenta is located, is known as the:","image":"","options":["Locule","Embryo sac","Micropyle","Thalamus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The ovarian cavity is called the locule, and the placenta lies inside it. The embryo sac is the female gametophyte, which lies within the nucellus of an ovule.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In an angiosperm whose leaf cells have 2n = 16 chromosomes, what are the chromosome numbers in an endosperm cell, the zygote and a synergid, respectively?","image":"","options":["16, 16, 8","24, 8, 16","24, 16, 8","8, 16, 24"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The endosperm comes from the triploid PEN, so it has 3n = 24 chromosomes. The zygote is diploid with 2n = 16. The synergid is a cell of the haploid female gametophyte, so it has n = 8.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Amorphophallus\nB. Yucca\nC. Zostera\nD. Maize (corn cob)\nList-II\nI. Long, ribbon-like pollen grains released and carried passively inside water\nII. Tallest flower, about 6 feet in height, offering a safe place to lay eggs\nIII. Moth larvae emerge from eggs laid in the ovary locule as the seeds begin developing\nIV. Stigma and style hang out and wave in the air to trap pollen\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Amorphophallus has the tallest flower (about 6 feet), and its reward is a safe egg-laying site. In Yucca, the moth lays eggs in the ovary locule and its larvae emerge as the seeds develop, so neither partner completes its life cycle without the other. Zostera releases ribbon-like pollen inside the water. The 'ears' of a corn cob are stigmas and styles that trap wind-borne pollen. The trap is mixing up the two egg-laying relationships.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The liquid 'water' of a tender coconut represents:","image":"","options":["Cellular endosperm","Free-nuclear endosperm","Persistent nucellus (perisperm)","Liquid part of the embryo"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Coconut water is free-nuclear endosperm made up of thousands of free nuclei. The surrounding white kernel is the cellular endosperm.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In rice and wheat, pollen grains lose viability within about 30 minutes of release, whereas in some members of Rosaceae, Leguminoseae and Solanaceae they remain viable for months.\nStatement II: The period of pollen viability is fixed for a species and does not depend on temperature or humidity.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I matches NCERT's examples. Statement II is wrong because pollen viability is highly variable and depends to some extent on the prevailing temperature and humidity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A typical mature angiosperm embryo sac is 8-nucleate but only 7-celled.\nReason (R): The two polar nuclei are not enclosed in separate cells but lie together in the single large central cell.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Six nuclei are walled off into separate cells: 3 antipodals, 2 synergids and 1 egg. The two polar nuclei share one central cell, giving 7 cells for 8 nuclei, so R directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following seeds does a remnant of the nucellus persist as perisperm?","image":"","options":["Castor","Black pepper","Groundnut","Pea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Perisperm is the residual, persistent nucellus. It is found in seeds such as black pepper and beet. Castor is the trap: it retains endosperm (albuminous), not nucellus, while pea and groundnut are non-albuminous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the advantages of seeds to angiosperms is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Seed formation is dependable because pollination and fertilisation in angiosperms are independent of water","Stored food reserves nourish the young seedling until it can photosynthesise on its own","Being products of sexual reproduction, seeds generate new genetic combinations leading to variation","Seeds are genetically identical to the parent, which keeps the species uniform as it colonises new areas"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Normal seeds come from sexual reproduction, so they produce new genetic combinations rather than being identical to the parent. Offspring that are clones of the parent arise only through apomixis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are haploid in a typical angiosperm ovule before fertilisation?\nNucellus cell, megaspore mother cell, functional megaspore, synergid, antipodal cell, egg cell, integument cell, each polar nucleus","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The functional megaspore, synergid, antipodal, egg and each polar nucleus are haploid, which makes five. The nucellus, MMC and integument are diploid sporophytic tissue. Treating each polar nucleus as diploid, or the central cell as triploid before fertilisation, is the usual error.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In apple and cashew, the thalamus contributes to fruit formation, so these are called false fruits.\nStatement II: Fruits that develop only from the ovary, with other floral parts degenerating, are called true fruits.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Both statements match NCERT. Fruits with a thalamus contribution, such as apple, strawberry and cashew, are false fruits. Most fruits come only from the ovary and are true fruits.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Fruits produced by applying growth hormones to induce parthenocarpy are seedless.\nReason (R): In parthenocarpy, ovules develop into seeds without fertilisation, as seen in apomixis.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Parthenocarpy is the development of fruit without fertilisation, and hormone-induced parthenocarpic fruits are seedless. Producing seeds without fertilisation is apomixis, a different phenomenon, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Pollen grains of many species can be stored for years for use in crop breeding programmes. They are kept in pollen banks at a temperature of about:","image":"","options":["$-20^{\\circ}C$ in a deep freezer","$4^{\\circ}C$ in a refrigerator","$-196^{\\circ}C$ in liquid nitrogen","$-80^{\\circ}C$ in dry ice"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Pollen grains can be stored for years in liquid nitrogen at $-196^{\\circ}C$. Pollen banks built this way work much like seed banks.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A Viola (common pansy) plant bears both chasmogamous and cleistogamous flowers. Which option correctly lists the kinds of pollination possible in each flower type?","image":"","options":["Chasmogamous: autogamy only; Cleistogamous: autogamy, geitonogamy and xenogamy","Chasmogamous: autogamy, geitonogamy and xenogamy; Cleistogamous: autogamy only","Chasmogamous: xenogamy only; Cleistogamous: geitonogamy only","Both flower types: autogamy, geitonogamy and xenogamy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chasmogamous flowers expose their anthers and stigma, so pollen can come from the same flower, another flower of the plant, or another plant. Cleistogamous flowers never open, so no outside pollen can enter and they are invariably autogamous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"An orange seed is squeezed and found to contain four embryos of different sizes. One arose from the zygote, and the other three arose from nucellar cells that protruded into the embryo sac. Which statement about these embryos is correct?","image":"","options":["All four embryos are genetically identical to one another because they lie in the same ovule","The nucellar embryos are diploid and genetically identical to the mother plant, whereas the zygotic embryo can carry new gene combinations","The nucellar embryos are haploid because they develop inside the embryo sac","The nucellar embryos are triploid because they develop in contact with the endosperm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Nucellar cells are diploid somatic cells of the mother plant. Embryos formed from them without fertilisation are therefore clones of the parent, which is the apomictic origin of polyembryony. The zygotic embryo results from fertilisation and can differ genetically.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In most flowering plants the embryo sac is said to show monosporic development. This means that:","image":"","options":["The embryo sac develops from only one of the four megaspores, while the other three degenerate","The megaspore mother cell develops directly into the embryo sac without meiosis","Only one embryo sac is present in the whole ovary","The embryo sac contains only one egg cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Monosporic development means a single functional megaspore forms the female gametophyte, and the other three megaspores degenerate. The term refers to the number of megaspores involved. It does not refer to the number of eggs or embryo sacs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a grass embryo, the portion of the embryonal axis above the level of attachment of the scutellum is the epicotyl.\nStatement II: The coleorrhiza is a hollow foliar structure that encloses the shoot apex and a few leaf primordia.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the epicotyl is the part of the axis above the scutellum's attachment. Statement II is incorrect because it describes the coleoptile. The coleorrhiza is an undifferentiated sheath around the radicle and root cap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of an angiosperm ovule represents its basal part and lies opposite the micropylar end?","image":"","options":["Funicle","Chalaza","Hilum","Integument"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The chalaza is the basal part of the ovule, located opposite the micropyle. The funicle is the stalk, and the hilum is the point where the funicle joins the ovule.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-SEXUAL-REPRO-PLANTS-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In an artificial hybridisation programme, the bag used to cover emasculated flowers is generally made of","image":"","options":["polythene","aluminium foil","muslin cloth","butter paper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that emasculated flowers are covered with a bag of suitable size, generally made of butter paper. The bag keeps unwanted pollen away from the stigma.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 1 · Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"}]});
